# System Architecture

TranZact ERP is a simplified, TranZact-inspired manufacturing ERP. A factory can see its stock, follow its orders, track what it is making, and read simple reports, all in one web app.

This document explains how the system is built and how its parts talk to each other. For screens and the style guide see [ui-design.md](ui-design.md). For team rules see [project-workflow.md](project-workflow.md). For the API list see [api-specification.md](api-specification.md) and for the tables see [database-design.md](database-design.md).

## 1. Technology stack

| Area | Technology |
| --- | --- |
| Frontend | React + Vite, JavaScript |
| UI styling | Tailwind CSS |
| Routing | React Router |
| API communication | Axios |
| Charts | Recharts |
| Backend | Node.js + Express.js |
| Database | PostgreSQL (SQL) |
| Authentication | JWT (JSON Web Token) + bcrypt password hashing |
| API testing | Postman |
| UI/UX design | Figma-style mockups (see [ui-design.md](ui-design.md)) |
| Version control | Git + GitHub |
| Frontend hosting | Vercel |
| Backend hosting | Render |
| Database hosting | Neon (PostgreSQL) |

JavaScript is the only programming language used in the project.

## 2. System architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U["User<br/>Admin / Manager / Worker"] --> FE

    subgraph Vercel["Vercel (frontend hosting)"]
        FE["React + Vite app<br/>Tailwind CSS, React Router,<br/>Axios, Recharts"]
    end

    subgraph Render["Render (backend hosting)"]
        direction TB
        MW["Middleware<br/>CORS, JWT check, role check"]
        RT["Routes"]
        CT["Controllers"]
        SV["Services<br/>stock rules"]
        MW --> RT --> CT --> SV
    end

    subgraph Neon["Neon (database hosting)"]
        DB[("PostgreSQL")]
    end

    FE -- "HTTPS + JSON<br/>Authorization: Bearer token" --> MW
    SV -- "SQL via pg" --> DB
    CT -- "SQL via pg" --> DB
```

The frontend and backend are separate applications. They talk to each other only through the REST API, using JSON. The browser never talks to the database directly.

## 3. How a request travels (login and a protected call)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    actor User
    participant FE as React app
    participant API as Express API
    participant DB as PostgreSQL

    User->>FE: Enter username and password
    FE->>API: POST /api/auth/login
    API->>DB: Find user, compare bcrypt hash
    DB-->>API: User and role
    API-->>FE: JWT token and user info
    FE->>FE: Save token and user in the browser

    User->>FE: Open Inventory page
    FE->>API: GET /api/items with Bearer token
    API->>API: Check token, check role
    API->>DB: SELECT items
    DB-->>API: Rows
    API-->>FE: JSON list
    FE-->>User: Inventory table
```

If the token is missing or wrong, the API answers `401`. If the role is not allowed, it answers `403 Forbidden`. All errors come back as `{ "error": "A clear message" }`.

## 4. Folder structure

```
tranzact-erp/
├── frontend/
│   └── src/
│       ├── components/    shared parts (Layout, Modal, StatusBadge ...)
│       ├── pages/         one file per screen
│       ├── modules/       inventory, orders, production, dashboard
│       ├── services/      Axios API helper
│       ├── hooks/
│       └── App.jsx
├── backend/
│   └── src/
│       ├── config/        database connection
│       ├── controllers/   request handlers
│       ├── routes/        URL to controller mapping
│       ├── middleware/    JWT check, role check
│       ├── services/      shared business rules (stock changes)
│       └── server.js
├── database/
│   ├── schema.sql         creates the tables
│   └── seed.sql           sample data and starting users
├── docs/                  all documentation (this folder)
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

`.env` files stay on each person's laptop and are never committed. A `.env.example` with empty values is shared instead.

## 5. Roles and permissions

Permissions are enforced in the backend. Hiding a button in the frontend only keeps the screen tidy.

| Action | Admin | Manager | Worker |
| --- | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| Log in | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| View items, stock history, low-stock list | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Add or edit item details | Yes | Yes | No |
| Change stock quantity (with a reason) | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| View orders and order timeline | Yes | Yes | No |
| Create orders, create invoice, dispatch or receive goods | Yes | Yes | No |
| View production orders | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Create production orders | Yes | Yes | No |
| Update production status and progress | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| View dashboard and reports | Yes | Yes | No |
| Manage users | Through seed data or pgAdmin | No | No |
| Delete records | There are no delete APIs | No | No |

There is no public registration page. The starting accounts (one Admin, one Manager, one Worker) come from `seed.sql`.

## 6. Data model

```mermaid
erDiagram
    ROLES ||--o{ USERS : has
    USERS ||--o{ STOCK_HISTORY : makes
    ITEMS ||--o{ STOCK_HISTORY : records
    ORDERS ||--|{ ORDER_ITEMS : contains
    ITEMS ||--o{ ORDER_ITEMS : "appears in"
    ITEMS ||--o{ PRODUCTION_ORDERS : "is made by"
    ITEMS ||--o{ BOM_LINES : "needs as material"
    ITEMS ||--o{ BOM_LINES : "is recipe for"
    PRODUCTION_ORDERS ||--o{ STOCK_HISTORY : changes
    ORDERS ||--o{ STOCK_HISTORY : changes

    ROLES {
        int id
        text name
    }
    USERS {
        int id
        text username
        text password_hash
        int role_id
    }
    ITEMS {
        int id
        text name
        text type "RAW or FINISHED"
        text unit
        numeric quantity
        numeric price
        numeric low_stock_limit
    }
    STOCK_HISTORY {
        int id
        int item_id
        numeric change_qty
        text changed_via
        text reason
    }
    ORDERS {
        int id
        text order_type "SALES or PURCHASE"
        text party_name
        date order_date
        date due_date
        text invoice_status
        text goods_status
    }
    ORDER_ITEMS {
        int id
        int order_id
        int item_id
        numeric quantity
        numeric price
    }
    PRODUCTION_ORDERS {
        int id
        int item_id
        numeric quantity
        text status
        date planned_date
    }
    BOM_LINES {
        int id
        int product_id
        int material_id
        numeric qty_per_unit
    }
```

Agreed names used everywhere (database, API and frontend):

| What | Agreed names |
| --- | --- |
| Tables | `users`, `roles`, `items`, `stock_history`, `orders`, `order_items`, `production_orders`, `bom_lines` |
| Item types | `RAW`, `FINISHED` |
| Order types | `SALES`, `PURCHASE` |
| Invoice status | Pending, Created |
| Goods status | Not Dispatched, Partially Dispatched, Dispatched |
| Production status | PLANNED, PENDING, WIP, IN-TESTING, COMPLETED (one step at a time, forward only) |
| Stock change types | Opening Stock, Manual Adjustment, Sales Dispatch, Purchase Inward, Production Consumption, Production Output |
| JSON field style | snake_case, same as the database columns (for example `low_stock_limit`, `goods_status`) |

## 7. Key business rules

1. **Every stock change is recorded.** Changing a quantity always writes a row in `stock_history` with the reason and who did it. Stock can never go below zero.
2. **Orders can be dispatched in parts.** A sales order can be partly dispatched several times. Stock goes down by the quantity dispatched each time, and the goods status moves from Not Dispatched to Partially Dispatched to Dispatched. Purchase orders work the same way for goods received, and stock goes up.
3. **Invoices have two states.** An order starts as Pending and becomes Created when the invoice is created.
4. **Production moves forward one step at a time.** PLANNED, then PENDING, then WIP, then IN-TESTING, then COMPLETED. It cannot skip or go back.
5. **Completing production updates stock in one step.** When an order becomes COMPLETED, the raw materials listed in the recipe (`bom_lines`) are used up and the finished goods are added to stock. This happens inside one database transaction, so it either fully succeeds or does nothing.
6. **Low stock.** An item is low when its quantity is below its `low_stock_limit`. It appears in the low-stock list and on the dashboard.
7. **Late orders.** An order is late when its due date has passed and its goods are not fully dispatched.

## 8. Main flow of the business

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    P["Purchase order<br/>raw material"] -->|goods received| RAW["Raw material stock"]
    RAW -->|used when COMPLETED| PR["Production order"]
    PR -->|output added| FIN["Finished goods stock"]
    FIN -->|goods dispatched| S["Sales order"]
    S --> INV["Invoice"]
```

## 9. Deployment

| Part | Where | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Database | Neon | Run `database/schema.sql`, then `database/seed.sql` once |
| Backend | Render | Set `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET`, `FRONTEND_URL` as environment variables |
| Frontend | Vercel | Set `VITE_API_URL` to the Render address followed by `/api`, then redeploy |

Things to remember:

- `FRONTEND_URL` on Render must be the exact Vercel address, with no slash at the end. This is how the backend decides which website may call it (CORS).
- Vite reads `VITE_API_URL` when the frontend is built, so after changing it the frontend must be redeployed.
- The free Render plan puts the backend to sleep when it is idle. The first request after a pause can take up to a minute. Open the live app a few minutes before a demo.
- A `vercel.json` file with a rewrite to `index.html` makes page refreshes work on any route.
